{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act statutory eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only for the statutory grounds in the Anti-Eviction Act, such as nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct or lease violations, and must serve the required notice; relocation assistance is required for no-fault evictions.","key_value":"6 times monthly rent relocation assistance (illegal-occupancy eviction)","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals, including single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings and complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Applies to most residential rentals; owner-occupied 2-3 unit premises with 2 or fewer rental units may be exempt."},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel, transient or seasonal guests (unless the occupant has no alternate residence and resides continually); units held in trust for a permanently occupying developmentally disabled family member.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes. This law also applies to rooming and boarding homes (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-53 to -84).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not remove a tenant, padlock the unit, disconnect utilities or block entry without a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant for removal is issued.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental premises in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"An illegally evicted tenant may recover possession and all damages, including court costs and reasonable attorney fees; the landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"Self-help evictions, or lockouts, made by the landlord are illegal in New Jersey.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Unconscionable rent increase limit (Anti-Eviction Act)","requirement":"A rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with all other laws or municipal ordinances, including rent control; a tenant who believes an increase is unconscionable may withhold the difference pending a court determination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The rent increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with all other laws or municipal ordinances, including rent control.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they apply.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
